  "IMG_1639": {
    "excluded": false,
    "reserved": false,
    "title": "Look east and then rise",
    "dimensions": "24x29",
    "material": "Oil, gypsum, epoxy",
    "price": "3000",
    "year": "2026",
    "notes": "Rather than the orbital sander and sandpaper used in the Declivity series, Zayan worked the surface with an angle grinder, carving bold strokes directly into the drywall. Left intentionally visible, these marks shift the emphasis toward gesture — and together with the color and subject matter, give the work an overall feel reminiscent of a Japanese woodblock print. The composition is organizedinto three distinct areas, each treated to underline its nature. In the upper section, tones of mauve and orange interlace rather thanoverlap the broad strokes, evoking a rising sun. The lower section reverses this logic: deep blue washes lay over the carved marks,creating depth and transforming the incisions into something resembling underwater reefs. Between them, the landmass occupies a central field, acting as a neutral balance between opposing forces. Backing: Steel, epoxy"
  },
